144
00:33:54.900 --> 00:34:10.580
danceratopz: So, thanks Nixo. Basically, I've been mucking about a little bit with, LLMs and EIP Markdown, and trying to see whether the complexity assessments that we do on the steel team can be automated and

145
00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:19.000
danceratopz: whether that data sort of gives us any signal that could help with scoping or with Devnets and whatever. And that's what I'd like to show you today.

146
00:34:19.290 --> 00:34:23.909
danceratopz: So what is a complexity assessment? Basically.

147
00:34:24.239 --> 00:34:43.209
danceratopz: a complexity assessment is like a questionnaire for people that love EIPs, and they can go through this questionnaire, and they can answer questions that tell you what this EIP changes about ethereum. But it's limited to the execution layer side at the moment. So these

148
00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:52.169
danceratopz: Basically, the goal is to try and sort of estimate how much effort it would take to implement the CIP, but scoped to the execution layer.

149
00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:56.350
danceratopz: And this has only existed since Glamsterdam.

150
00:34:56.600 --> 00:35:07.800
danceratopz: Where Mario, like, created this template with 24 questions or criteria. And you can give a score from 0 to 72 to some EIP that you get as input.

151
00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:14.230
danceratopz: And we did this for Glamsterdam, human evaluations at that time, of course, it was already AI-assisted.

152
00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:16.980
danceratopz: And

153
00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:25.239
danceratopz: what would what would one of these questions look like? You've got example in the bottom right hand corner. So like, basically, this is a quite simple example.

154
00:35:26.510 --> 00:35:40.239
danceratopz: did we add in? Did the CIP add any new opcodes? And then you can score it based on whether that was the case, and how complicated these opcodes were, or how many there were. And then you've got some kind of indication of how hard that would be to implement and test.

155
00:35:40.960 --> 00:35:41.750
danceratopz: Umm.

156
00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:55.520
danceratopz: It seemed that the first version of the template had a few pitfalls. And for example, it didn't really take into account across the IP interaction very well. And Mario did a sort of retrospective.

157
00:35:56.440 --> 00:36:00.340
danceratopz: like evaluation of what the Glamsterdam EIP testing did.

158
00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:06.510
danceratopz: and then calibrated the original template against the effort that he saw in in our repo.

159
00:36:06.630 --> 00:36:11.819
danceratopz: And so this added four new criteria, and the range grew.

160
00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:16.989
danceratopz: But in particular, it catches, captures cross EIP interaction as well.

161
00:36:19.180 --> 00:36:20.210
danceratopz: And.

162
00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:39.180
danceratopz: like Mario and I were talking about this and like wondering if it's like actually useful. I mean, who knows? Like, you know, it's quite hard to sort of assess these things. How how much signal does these does this thing give? And we sort of vibed a little bit and had the idea that what we could do is we could use LLMs to basically do a retrospective study.

163
00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:47.039
danceratopz: of these EIPs, sorry, of all EIPs and all previous forks, in order to really see whether.

164
00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:59.109
danceratopz: we can somehow see that the amount of effort that this EIP questionnaire tells us, like, does it really, like, represent how much time it takes to ship the EIP?

165
00:37:00.190 --> 00:37:01.360
danceratopz: And…

166
00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:11.979
danceratopz: So I think that's pretty — so yeah, basically, I'll just say I just reran this late last night, and I reran it with Claude.

167
00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:21.910
danceratopz: And it's ran, like… so that it may… Claude will basically, like, evaluate any IP, and for the retrospective assessments, it tries… I've picked dates.

168
00:37:22.190 --> 00:37:30.230
danceratopz: from the EIPs markdown and commit refs, which represent when, like, fork development started or scoping stopped.

169
00:37:30.610 --> 00:37:39.219
danceratopz: and took a snapshot of the IP. And asked Claude to do his best to not apply his knowledge afterwards in order to evaluate it at that point in time.

170
00:37:39.530 --> 00:37:42.210
danceratopz: So somehow sandboxed. It's not perfect, but

171
00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:45.709
danceratopz: It will have been trained on that data, but it is what it is.

172
00:37:48.220 --> 00:37:51.719
danceratopz: And when I did this study like 6 weeks ago with like

173
00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:54.799
danceratopz: Claude or something, and,

174
00:37:54.940 --> 00:38:11.709
danceratopz: it seemed to over evaluate a lot of things, and it seemed to get things really blatantly wrong. So I just I've made a proposal to improve the template again, but it keeps the criteria so it shouldn't really change what human rights, but it tries to disambiguate the language so that an LLM. Can be a bit more precise about its evaluation.

175
00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:20.389
danceratopz: You can have a look in more detail afterwards, but it's just basically trying to keep the wording a bit more,

176
00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:27.080
danceratopz: a bit more precise to avoid disambiguity for the robots.

177
00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:43.239
danceratopz: So I think, at this point, I will go away from my slides, because the site's a bit nicer, and so basically what I… I posed the question, does, like, the complexity… the accumulated complexity for the entire fork, does it somehow track

178
00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:57.840
danceratopz: the time to mainnet and you can see if you count. So from all these evaluations you get all these scores, you get a score per EIP, and then you can accumulate all these scores and add them all up, and you get a tally.

