01:29:07.300 --> 01:29:10.170
Danno Ferrin: So a couple of questions there.

561
01:29:10.330 --> 01:29:30.160
Danno Ferrin: So, first, the overarching… my overarching motivation for this is cryptographic agility. We need to have multiple signature families available for people to put their funds in. We can't… locking in on just one is, is, is incredibly risky, in my opinion. As far as why all three, because people need choices. We can lock down into one of them.

562
01:29:30.250 --> 01:29:47.940
Danno Ferrin: But to directly address Kevin's question, what's the cost of doing this in the EVM? It's about a million, and they've had… it started out at 5 million, and it's gone down about a million, and that includes, a lot of AI performance improvements to get the cost of doing this evaluation in raw EVM.

563
01:29:48.110 --> 01:30:07.600
Danno Ferrin: And that runs into a couple of major problems, because 4337 limits, validation to 500,000 gas, and 3160, which is BASIS, response to frame limits validation to 100,000 gas. So, doing this in EVM-only bytecode locks out two of the major account abstraction solutions that are going to be out there.

564
01:30:07.670 --> 01:30:14.610
Danno Ferrin: So that's one of the reasons why I think we need to look at specifying what it would look like if it were to be.

565
01:30:14.780 --> 01:30:15.960
Danno Ferrin: a precompile

566
01:30:16.000 --> 01:30:29.550
Danno Ferrin: One of the strong arguments brought against it is, well, look at what happened with Blake, and look at what happened with BLS. Nobody's using those. Look at, you know, BLS, you know, nobody's using those, it's relatively low impact. But I think that's the wrong comparison. What we need to look at is P256.

567
01:30:29.560 --> 01:30:44.469
Danno Ferrin: And that has been, brought around… that has been brought up over 8 to 10 years before it was finally adopted in various levels to various people. The reason it was brought up is because it's the one that financial regulators are asking all of their clients to use.

568
01:30:44.630 --> 01:30:49.959
Danno Ferrin: So, one potential middle ground for this, is maybe not to ship it directly.

569
01:30:49.980 --> 01:31:07.649
Danno Ferrin: in Hegatop, but instead to reserve one of the 100 series precompile ranges, like was done for P256. Get a specification for it, get the tests written into the Ethereum compliance tests, so that if it were shipped, this is what it would look like, it's known, this is the address, it is standardized.

570
01:31:07.650 --> 01:31:12.600
Danno Ferrin: And that'll avoid the problem we have with P256, which is where there are about 2 or 3 different definitions.

571
01:31:12.600 --> 01:31:32.539
Danno Ferrin: There are different, reactions to the various, you know, how do you respond when the answer's no? Do you respond with a hash? Do you not respond with a hash? The standardization, putting it in, like, the 100 series makes it a lot easier to, if the… if Ethereum may not decide suddenly it does need it later, to just turn it on. It also creates space

572
01:31:32.540 --> 01:31:42.379
